        "original_id": "1f423d9b-d6c5-5e5b-8b9f-4d21edf87a05",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Yealand Redmayne, North West England, Lancashire, Kendal, south Cumbria, Westmorland, River Ribble\n- Systems: Lancaster Canal, Leeds and Liverpool Canal\n- Structures: bridges, milestone\n\nFacts:\n- The Lancaster Canal was originally planned to run from Westhoughton in Lancashire to Kendal in south Cumbria, then in Westmorland.\n- The section of the Lancaster Canal around the crossing of the River Ribble was never completed.\n- Much of the southern end of the Lancaster Canal was leased to the Leeds and Liverpool Canal, and it is now generally considered part of that canal.\n- The Lancaster Canal passes through the parish of Yealand Redmayne.\n- In Yealand Redmayne, two bridges crossing the Lancaster Canal are listed buildings.\n- In Yealand Redmayne, a milestone on the Lancaster Canal towpath is a listed building.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "1b49c67d-04e1-512d-b927-f4d6f3c1cdc7",
        "text": "Page: Listed buildings in Yealand Redmayne. Sentence: The Lancaster Canal passes through the parish, and two bridges crossing it are listed, together with a milestone on its towpath.\nPage: Lancaster Canal. Sentence: The Lancaster Canal is a canal in North West England, originally planned to run from Westhoughton in Lancashire to Kendal in south Cumbria (then in Westmorland).\nPage: Lancaster Canal. Sentence: The section around the crossing of the River Ribble was never completed, and much of the southern end leased to the Leeds and Liverpool Canal, of which it is now generally considered part.",
